    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm studying for my circuits final and im a bit stuck. can u explain the difference between how a capacitor behaves in a dc circuit vs an ac circuit? also what's the formula for capacitive reactance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In an DC circuit, capacitors eventually stop charging and reach steady-state, acting like an open circuit after being fully charged. In an AC circuit, they continuously charge and discharge due to the frequency of the alternating current, allowing them to pass AC while limiting DC, emerging as a low-pass filter. Capacitive reactance (Xc) is calculated as Xc = 1/(2πfC), with \"f\" being the frequency of the AC and \"C\" representing the capacitance. Higher frequencies result in lower capacitive reactance, and vice versa.",
